    3361	c since `c/k = 1` requires `k = c` not a prime cover-step). So unburnt
    3362	= V \\ {1}. Firing it once: each prime p has `(deg(p) − 1)` neighbors
    3363	in V \\ {1} (all except 1), so net change at p is `−1`, giving
    3364	`D'(p) = 0`; each composite c has all `deg(c)` neighbors in V \\ {1}
    3365	(since 1 ∉ adj(c)), net change is 0; q gains 1 from each fired prime,
    3366	giving `D'(1) = π(N)`. The result is non-negative on V \\ {1} and
    3367	trivially superstable, hence q-reduced. ∎
