    886	| §F6 / §P7 — does the dyadic family π(2^k), k = 1..N, admit per-query polylog amortisation beyond generic-magnitude HKM? (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES F6 / OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS P7 per-query side) | EXPERIMENT (B-NEGATIVE: three independent structural tests on (π(2^k))_{k=1..56} all return random-baseline values within Monte Carlo noise) | E (E1.1 + E1.5; refines E1.1 inline) | S246 novelty mode. Built `experiments/wildcard/dyadic_pi_structure/dyadic_pi_structure.py`: (1) hard-coded OEIS A007053 reference values π(2^k) for k=0..56, verified k≤18 against sympy.primepi (18/18 exact); (2) computed Li(2^k) and Riemann R(2^k) at 80-decimal mpmath precision, residual r_R(k) = π(2^k) − R(2^k), normalised c_R(k) = r_R(k)/2^{k/2}; (3) Monte Carlo random-binary BM linear-complexity null at length 56 with matched n_pos=25 / n_neg=31 split (4000 shuffles): mean=28.25, std=1.01, p05=27, p95=30; data BM(sign(r_R))=28, sits at 0.25σ below MC mean — F1 (BM ≤ MC p05) FAILS; (4) MC iid-Gaussian lag-1 |autocorr| null (4000 trials): p95=0.250, p99=0.328, p999=0.437; data max |ac| over lags 1..10 = 0.283 at lag 1, below MC p99, Bonferroni-corrected over 10 lags p ≈ 0.4 — F2 (max |ac| ≥ MC p999) FAILS; (5) HKM cost test via fresh subprocess sympy.primepi with no sieve-cache reuse: median T(2^k) / mean(T(2^k − 1), T(2^k + 1)) = 0.998 / 1.047 / 0.995 at k = 28, 30, 32 (avg 1.013) — F3 (dyadic speedup ratio ≤ 0.5) FAILS. **All three F-claims FAIL → B-NEGATIVE per-query closure.** Note: an earlier v1 result with Li-baseline gave spurious lag-1 = 0.87 driven by the smooth -Li(√x)/2 Möbius-series correction; switching to R-baseline (which absorbs the smooth Σ μ(n)/n Li(x^{1/n}) series) eliminates the confound. **Structural reason** (two independent mechanisms): (a) information-theoretic — the explicit-formula zero-sum −Σ_ρ R(x^ρ) ≈ −2√x Σ_n cos(γ_n log x − arg(ρ_n))/|ρ_n| has phases γ_n · k log 2 mod 2π Weyl-equidistributed in k for every γ_n with γ_n irrational, independently of the dyadic form; (b) algorithmic — Lucy / Meissel-Lehmer outer loop processes {n ≤ √x} and x^{2/3}-smooth counts which carry no binary-representation side-channel. **What this rules out**: (i) any algorithm computing π(2^k) substantially faster than generic-x π(x) at the same magnitude; (ii) any compressed representation of (π(2^k))_k of size below the trivial Σ_k k = O(N²) output bit-budget; (iii) any short-recurrence/closed-form for residuals r_R(k) at the n=56 scale. **What this does NOT close**: cross-k batched amortisation (Thread-5 / Correlation-Dichotomy shape transposed to dyadic family) — the shared zero database (γ_n)_{n=1..K} is k-independent and admits per-query O(K) cost with K = polylog. Registered as F6.b in NOVELTY_CHALLENGES.md and P7.b in OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md. **Successor challenges proposed in S246**: F6.a (cross-modulus generalisation x_k = m^k for m ∈ {3, 5, 6, 10, 30}), F6.b (batched-on-k amortisation), F6.c (rigorous Ω(N²) lower bound from output bit-budget). **Cross-domain ingredient**: Berlekamp-Massey linear complexity over GF(2) (signal-processing standard). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md addition: "Berlekamp-Massey + Monte Carlo random-shuffle null for testing low-complexity linear recurrences in arithmetic sequences — USED-E (S246), confirmed n=56 too short to discriminate polylog from random; minimum useful length n > log²(n) · 5 ≈ 200". **Cites** E1.1 (per-query HKM x^{2/3} bound; refined inline with dyadic per-query no-amortisation note), E1.5 (per-step 0.537 entropy; consistent with Weyl-equidistribution mechanism). See `experiments/wildcard/dyadic_pi_structure/{dyadic_pi_structure.py, dyadic_pi_structure_results.md, raw_data.json, hkm_cost.json, summary.json}`, `archive/sessions/session246_f6_dyadic_pi_structure.md`. | 246 |
    887	| §P.P2 batched π_h(x) on h, slot 2 — does the within-window cross-x ensemble of HL residuals r_h(x) = π_h(x) − S_h·li_2(x) at fixed (anchor, h) follow a half-Gaussian shape (Thread 7 slot 2 transposition); is there a stable F_GUE-like factor σ_eff/σ_pred? | EXPERIMENT (B-MIXED: cross-x within-window FAILS half-Gaussian KS due to random-walk-like correlation; cross-h at fixed x PASSES with median KS p ≈ 0.7-0.8; σ-suppression ratio in [0.36, 0.70] but NOT decade-stable like Thread 7's F_GUE = 0.755) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S224 + S240..S244 + S418 — methodological finding identifies cross-h as the natural ensemble for HL residual analysis; refines slot-1 dichotomy into ensemble-aware form) | Thread 8 commit step 2/4 (S419). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_pi_h/slot2_multisample.py` (~340 lines): batched-sieve via numpy AND-shift cumsum (memory-efficient; 720 rows in 21s total wall time). Three anchors x ∈ {10⁶, 10⁷, 10⁸} × N=30 log-uniform x_j in quarter-decade [x_anchor, x_anchor·10^{0.25}] × 8 representative h ∈ {2, 6, 14, 22, 30, 42, 90, 198} (S_h ∈ [1.32, 4.0]). Three statistical aggregates: (A) per-(anchor, h) cross-x KS (24 cells, N=30 each); (B) per-(anchor, x_j) cross-h KS (90 cells, N=8 each); (C) pooled per-anchor (N=240 each). **Headline finding**: cross-x at fixed (anchor, h) FAILS half-Gaussian KS — median p_eff = 0.033, only 8/24 cells with p > 0.1. Cells that fail show median/σ_eff ≈ mean/σ_eff ≈ 0.95 (theoretical half-Gaussian = 0.6745/0.7979) — clustered |r| values, not Gaussian-tailed. Within-window range diagnostic: (max|r| − min|r|)/σ_eff has mean ≈ 1.5 across cells; residuals drift but don't decohere. **Structural diagnosis**: HL_h(x) = S_h · li_2(x) is analytic-smooth, π_h(x) is step; the residual is a slowly-varying random-walk in x (drift O(σ_eff) within quarter-decade, no oscillation), unlike Thread 7's zero-truncation residual which oscillates with wavelength ~1/log x. Cross-x sampling within a small log-window does NOT give iid samples for HL residuals. **Cross-h at fixed (anchor, x_j) PASSES half-Gaussian KS**: median p_eff = 0.840 / 0.698 / 0.804 at anchors 10⁶/10⁷/10⁸; **all 90/90 cells with p > 0.1**. The cross-h ensemble is the natural sampling for HL residual shape analysis. **σ-suppression ratio σ_eff/σ_pred_pois = 0.36-0.70** across 24 (anchor, h) cells, where σ_pred_pois = √(S_h·li_2(x)) is the HL random-residual Poisson prediction. Empirical RMS is consistently SMALLER than Poisson — analogue of Thread 7's GUE pair-correlation suppression (correlations among primes via singular series itself reduce the variance below random-residual). NOT decade-stable: 0.50 → 0.70 → 0.41 across 10⁶/10⁷/10⁸; the 10⁷ bump driven by anomaly cell (10⁷, h=6) cousin-prime σ_eff/σ_pois = 1.64 (signed range = 481, persistent large residual episode). Excluding h=6: ratio narrows to ~0.36-0.43, still mildly drifting with x. **Pooled per-anchor (N=240) KS**: borderline at 10⁶ (p=0.082), reject at 10⁷ (p<0.001, h=6 outlier), borderline at 10⁸ (p=0.217). Half-Gaussian shape holds at the cell level but pooled hides the anomaly. **What this rules out**: (i) the original Thread-7-direct-transposition hypothesis "HL has stable F_GUE-like σ_eff/σ_pred ≈ 0.74 across decades" — directly tested, ratio drifts in [0.36, 0.70]; (ii) cross-x sampling within quarter-decade as a fair iid ensemble for HL residual statistics — methodologically rejected. **What this does NOT close**: (i) cross-h ensemble at fixed x as a path to a precise σ-formula for HL — slot 2 demonstrates shape but does not derive σ_eff(x, h) closed form; (ii) Q-truncated singular series cost-vs-error tradeoff — slot 3; (iii) anchor extension to 10⁹/10¹⁰ to test whether σ_eff/σ_pois stabilises — slot 3 side measurement; (iv) named-exponent error bound for polylog HL evaluator — slot 3-4 wrap. **Methodological warning** (preserved for slot 3+ design): within-window cross-x sampling is misleading for HL residual analysis; use cross-h at fixed x. **Cross-domain